<section class="panel">
  <h2>Words we use</h2>
  <p class="sub">You do not need to read a website’s technical files. This is what the tables mean.</p>
  <dl class="glossary">
    <div>
      <dt>robots.txt</dt>
      <dd>A public note on a website that says which automated visitors may look around. We read that note. We do not read the articles, products, or comments.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Named</dt>
      <dd>The site wrote the bot’s actual name, such as GPTBot or ClaudeBot. That is a decision about that bot.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Block all bots</dt>
      <dd>The site said no to everyone, without naming a bot. We still count that. GPTBot is blocked, but it is a blanket policy — not a GPTBot-specific one. It is shown in purple, not pink.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Blocked / allowed / mixed</dt>
      <dd>Blocked means no. Allowed means yes. Mixed means some pages yes, some pages no.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Training vs AI search</dt>
      <dd>Training is using pages to build a model. AI search is using pages to answer a question or cite a source. A site can refuse one and allow the other.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>When a person asks</dt>
      <dd>The bot fetches a page because a human asked to see that link — not because it is vacuuming the site.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Opt-out signal</dt>
      <dd>A switch such as Google-Extended. It is not a crawler. Do not add it into “how many bots were blocked.”</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>
